From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 4 Sep 2026 22:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wlroots: compositor: re-import a texture lost with its
 renderer

A wlr_client_buffer's texture belongs to the renderer that imported it.
When that renderer is destroyed -- which is exactly what a compositor does
after a GPU reset, via wlr_renderer_destroy() and
wlr_compositor_set_renderer() -- client_buffer_handle_renderer_destroy()
sets client_buffer->texture to NULL so nothing is left dangling.

Nothing ever puts a texture back. wlr_compositor_set_renderer() only swaps
the pointer and the destroy listener; the only path that creates a client
buffer is surface_apply_damage(), which runs on a client commit. So after a
reset every surface keeps a wlr_client_buffer whose texture is NULL, and
wlr_surface_get_texture() returns NULL for it until that client happens to
commit again.

A client that animates commits within a frame or two and recovers on its
own. A static layer-surface does not commit at all: the wallpaper, the
phosh panel and the on-screen keyboard stay textureless for the rest of the
session, and the compositor draws nothing where they are. Measured on a
Pixel 2 XL (msm8998, Adreno 540, mainline 7.2), where the A540 is reset
several times a session under a browser load: with phoc's
"repaint every output after recreating the renderer" fix in place the
compositor correctly repaints, and correctly paints those surfaces blank.

The source buffer survives the reset -- a dmabuf's fds and a shm pool's
mapping are both still valid, and client_buffer->source is NULL only if the
buffer itself was destroyed. So import it again. Doing it lazily in
wlr_surface_get_texture() puts the recovery on the one path every consumer
of a surface texture already goes through, and only for surfaces that are
actually drawn.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 types/wlr_compositor.c | 34 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+)

diff --git a/subprojects/wlroots-0.20.x/types/wlr_compositor.c b/subprojects/wlroots-0.20.x/types/wlr_compositor.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/subprojects/wlroots-0.20.x/types/wlr_compositor.c
+++ b/subprojects/wlroots-0.20.x/types/wlr_compositor.c
@@ -813,10 +813,44 @@ struct wlr_surface *wlr_surface_create(struct wlr_compositor *compositor,
 	return surface;
 }
 
+/**
+ * Import the surface's buffer into the compositor's current renderer again.
+ *
+ * A client buffer's texture belongs to the renderer that imported it and is
+ * dropped when that renderer is destroyed, which is what happens on a GPU
+ * reset. The source buffer is still there, so import it again instead of
+ * leaving the surface without a texture until the client next commits: a
+ * client that animates recovers on its own, but a static layer-surface such
+ * as a wallpaper, a panel or an on-screen keyboard never commits again and
+ * would stay blank for the rest of the session.
+ */
+static void surface_reimport_buffer(struct wlr_surface *surface) {
+	struct wlr_buffer *source = surface->buffer->source;
+	if (source == NULL || surface->compositor->renderer == NULL) {
+		return;
+	}
+
+	wlr_buffer_lock(source);
+	struct wlr_client_buffer *buffer =
+		wlr_client_buffer_create(source, surface->compositor->renderer);
+	wlr_buffer_unlock(source);
+	if (buffer == NULL) {
+		wlr_log(WLR_ERROR, "Failed to re-import buffer");
+		return;
+	}
+
+	wlr_buffer_unlock(&surface->buffer->base);
+	surface->buffer = buffer;
+	wlr_log(WLR_INFO, "Re-imported a surface buffer into the new renderer");
+}
+
 struct wlr_texture *wlr_surface_get_texture(struct wlr_surface *surface) {
 	if (surface->buffer == NULL) {
 		return NULL;
 	}
+	if (surface->buffer->texture == NULL) {
+		surface_reimport_buffer(surface);
+	}
 	return surface->buffer->texture;
 }
 
